# ~/.brushrc — brush-specific settings only
#
# brush reads ~/.bashrc first (unchanged), then this file. So everything shared
# between bash and brush belongs in .bashrc; only put things here that should
# apply to brush and NOT to bash.
#
# WHY THERE IS NO config.toml
# brush also supports ~/.config/brush/config.toml, and this repo deliberately
# does not use it. The schema is undocumented, and brush silently accepts
# unknown keys — feeding it `bogus_key = true` loads without complaint. A
# config.toml full of plausible-looking keys would therefore be an invisible
# no-op that reads like working configuration. Revisit if upstream documents it.
#
# The interactive feature flags (--enable-zsh-hooks, --enable-highlighting) are
# set on Ghostty's `command` line, not here — they are startup flags with no
# config-file equivalent.

# Keep this file quiet. If you find yourself adding much, ask whether it belongs
# in .bashrc so bash benefits too.

# Nothing brush-specific is needed yet. Intentionally near-empty: the point of
# brush is that your existing bash config works as-is.
#
# Deliberately NOT set here:
#   PS1  — starship owns the prompt, and it is initialised in .bashrc for both
#          shells. Setting PS1 here would be overwritten by starship anyway.
#   vi mode / jj binding — both live in .bashrc so bash gets them too.
